---
const before=Array.from({length:6},()=>['live','dead','dead','dead']);
const after=Array.from({length:6},()=>['live','free','free','free']);
const rewritten=[['live','live','live','live'],['live','live','free','free']];
const strips=[{label:'Before cleanup',detail:'6 live units + 18 obsolete units · 6 pages',pages:before},{label:'After ordinary vacuum',detail:'6 live units + 18 reusable units · 6 pages',pages:after},{label:'After a justified rewrite',detail:'The same 6 live units · 2 pages',pages:rewritten}];
---
<figure class="vac-figure" aria-labelledby="vac-space-title"><figcaption id="vac-space-title"><span>Figure 2</span> Free bytes do not remove the pages around them</figcaption><div class="vac-scene">
{strips.map(strip=><div class="vac-space-strip"><b>{strip.label}</b><p>{strip.detail}</p><div class="vac-pages">{strip.pages.map((page,i)=><div class="vac-page"><small>Page {i+1}</small><div>{page.map(kind=><span class:list={['vac-unit',kind]}>{kind==='live'?'Live':kind==='dead'?'Old':'Free'}</span>)}</div></div>)}</div></div>)}
</div><p class="vac-caption">A separate schematic heap: six pages, each with four equal-sized units. The first row’s old versions are all removable. Ordinary vacuum frees their bytes within each page; every page still holds live data. A rewrite packs that same data into fewer pages. Units, packing and counts are invented, not PostgreSQL tuple sizes or measured bloat.</p></figure>
